{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "IDOR in Event Category Updates",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IDOR in Event Category Updates"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The endpoint for updating an event (`PUT /api/admin/events/[id]`) does not verify that the categories being updated actually belong to the event. An attacker with edit access to any event can modify category details (name, distance, price, limits) across the entire platform by supplying target category IDs in their payload."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The endpoint for updating an event (`PUT /api/admin/events/[id]`) does not verify that the categories being updated actually belong to the event. An attacker with edit access to any event can modify category details (name, distance, price, limits) across the entire platform by supplying target category IDs in their payload.\n\nAn attacker with `event:edit` access on a single event can modify the details (name, distance, price, slot limits, inclusions) of any category belonging to any other event or organization. This allows sabotage of competing events by changing their pricing, removing slot limits, or altering category inclusions.\n\nUpdate the category loop in the `PUT` handler to use `prisma.category.updateMany` with `eventId: id` in the `where` clause. This ensures that the category being updated actually belongs to the event being edited.",
                "markdown": "The endpoint for updating an event (`PUT /api/admin/events/[id]`) does not verify that the categories being updated actually belong to the event. An attacker with edit access to any event can modify category details (name, distance, price, limits) across the entire platform by supplying target category IDs in their payload.\n\nAn attacker with `event:edit` access on a single event can modify the details (name, distance, price, slot limits, inclusions) of any category belonging to any other event or organization. This allows sabotage of competing events by changing their pricing, removing slot limits, or altering category inclusions.\n\nUpdate the category loop in the `PUT` handler to use `prisma.category.updateMany` with `eventId: id` in the `where` clause. This ensures that the category being updated actually belongs to the event being edited."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "PayMongo Webhook Signature Authentication Bypass via Missing Header",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PayMongo Webhook Signature Authentication Bypass via Missing Header"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PayMongo webhook endpoint (`/api/webhooks/paymongo`) skips signature verification if the `paymongo-signature` header is omitted, allowing attackers to forge successful payment events and mark arbitrary registrations as PAID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The PayMongo webhook endpoint (`/api/webhooks/paymongo`) skips signature verification if the `paymongo-signature` header is omitted, allowing attackers to forge successful payment events and mark arbitrary registrations as PAID.\n\nAn attacker can completely bypass payment for any registration by forging a webhook payload pointing to their `orderRef` or `checkoutSessionId`, granting them free access to events and resulting in direct financial loss for the organizers.\n\nEnsure that if the `PAYMONGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is configured, the `paymongo-signature` header is strictly required. If the header is missing, the request should be rejected immediately.",
                "markdown": "The PayMongo webhook endpoint (`/api/webhooks/paymongo`) skips signature verification if the `paymongo-signature` header is omitted, allowing attackers to forge successful payment events and mark arbitrary registrations as PAID.\n\nAn attacker can completely bypass payment for any registration by forging a webhook payload pointing to their `orderRef` or `checkoutSessionId`, granting them free access to events and resulting in direct financial loss for the organizers.\n\nEnsure that if the `PAYMONGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is configured, the `paymongo-signature` header is strictly required. If the header is missing, the request should be rejected immediately."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference on Category ID allows Payment Bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure Direct Object Reference on Category ID allows Payment Bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The checkout routes (`/api/checkout` and `/api/checkout/manual`) fail to validate whether the `categoryId` provided for a participant actually belongs to the specified `eventId`. If an attacker supplies a `categoryId` belonging to a different event, the price calculation defaults to 0, allowing them to register while paying only the minimal administrative fee (or 0 if waived)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The checkout routes (`/api/checkout` and `/api/checkout/manual`) fail to validate whether the `categoryId` provided for a participant actually belongs to the specified `eventId`. If an attacker supplies a `categoryId` belonging to a different event, the price calculation defaults to 0, allowing them to register while paying only the minimal administrative fee (or 0 if waived).\n\nAn attacker can bypass paying the race entry fee (which could be thousands of PHP) and register for high-value events while only paying the negligible platform fee. This represents a significant financial loss and business logic bypass.\n\nIn both checkout routes, iterate over the `participants` array and explicitly assert that every `categoryId` is present in the `event.categories` array fetched from the database. Reject the request with a 400 Bad Request if any category is invalid.",
                "markdown": "The checkout routes (`/api/checkout` and `/api/checkout/manual`) fail to validate whether the `categoryId` provided for a participant actually belongs to the specified `eventId`. If an attacker supplies a `categoryId` belonging to a different event, the price calculation defaults to 0, allowing them to register while paying only the minimal administrative fee (or 0 if waived).\n\nAn attacker can bypass paying the race entry fee (which could be thousands of PHP) and register for high-value events while only paying the negligible platform fee. This represents a significant financial loss and business logic bypass.\n\nIn both checkout routes, iterate over the `participants` array and explicitly assert that every `categoryId` is present in the `event.categories` array fetched from the database. Reject the request with a 400 Bad Request if any category is invalid."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-20",
              "name": "Empty Participants Array allows Free Ghost Registration Creation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Empty Participants Array allows Free Ghost Registration Creation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The checkout routes do not validate that the `participants` array contains at least one runner. Submitting an empty array results in a subtotal of 0 and a platform fee of 0. The server considers this a free order, bypasses PayMongo entirely, and creates a PAID registration with no actual runners."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The checkout routes do not validate that the `participants` array contains at least one runner. Submitting an empty array results in a subtotal of 0 and a platform fee of 0. The server considers this a free order, bypasses PayMongo entirely, and creates a PAID registration with no actual runners.\n\nAn attacker can pollute the registration database with unlimited free, empty orders. This clutters the admin dashboard, interferes with analytics, and consumes some database resources, although it does not consume actual event slots since no runner records are created.\n\nVerify that the `participants` array has a length greater than zero before performing any logic or calculations. Return a 400 Bad Request if it is empty.",
                "markdown": "The checkout routes do not validate that the `participants` array contains at least one runner. Submitting an empty array results in a subtotal of 0 and a platform fee of 0. The server considers this a free order, bypasses PayMongo entirely, and creates a PAID registration with no actual runners.\n\nAn attacker can pollute the registration database with unlimited free, empty orders. This clutters the admin dashboard, interferes with analytics, and consumes some database resources, although it does not consume actual event slots since no runner records are created.\n\nVerify that the `participants` array has a length greater than zero before performing any logic or calculations. Return a 400 Bad Request if it is empty."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-20",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-613",
              "name": "Incomplete Logout / Failure to Invalidate JWT on Server Side",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Incomplete Logout / Failure to Invalidate JWT on Server Side"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The logout functionality only clears the `admin_token` cookie on the client side, but fails to invalidate the stateless JWT on the server side. An intercepted or stolen JWT can still be used after the user has explicitly logged out."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The logout functionality only clears the `admin_token` cookie on the client side, but fails to invalidate the stateless JWT on the server side. An intercepted or stolen JWT can still be used after the user has explicitly logged out.\n\nIf a user's JWT is stolen (e.g. via XSS, network interception, or local access), the attacker can continue to access the account until the token expires, even if the legitimate user attempts to terminate the session by logging out.\n\nUpdate the logout route to invalidate the session on the server side. Since the application uses a stateless JWT without individual session IDs, the most straightforward fix using the existing schema is to update the user's `sessionsValidFrom` timestamp to the current time, identical to the \"Sign out other devices\" functionality. This ensures the JWT is rejected by `getActor()` on subsequent requests.",
                "markdown": "The logout functionality only clears the `admin_token` cookie on the client side, but fails to invalidate the stateless JWT on the server side. An intercepted or stolen JWT can still be used after the user has explicitly logged out.\n\nIf a user's JWT is stolen (e.g. via XSS, network interception, or local access), the attacker can continue to access the account until the token expires, even if the legitimate user attempts to terminate the session by logging out.\n\nUpdate the logout route to invalidate the session on the server side. Since the application uses a stateless JWT without individual session IDs, the most straightforward fix using the existing schema is to update the user's `sessionsValidFrom` timestamp to the current time, identical to the \"Sign out other devices\" functionality. This ensures the JWT is rejected by `getActor()` on subsequent requests."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-613",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/613.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-602",
              "name": "Negative Transaction Fee Allows Payment Bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Negative Transaction Fee Allows Payment Bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The checkout routes blindly trust the `transactionFee` (or `transactionFeeCents`) value provided by the client when calculating the total order amount. Because the application does not validate that the transaction fee is non-negative, an attacker can submit a negative transaction fee to offset the cost of the event. When choosing an e-wallet like GCash or PayMaya, this manipulated, vastly reduced total amount is passed directly to the PayMongo Payment Intents API."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The checkout routes blindly trust the `transactionFee` (or `transactionFeeCents`) value provided by the client when calculating the total order amount. Because the application does not validate that the transaction fee is non-negative, an attacker can submit a negative transaction fee to offset the cost of the event. When choosing an e-wallet like GCash or PayMaya, this manipulated, vastly reduced total amount is passed directly to the PayMongo Payment Intents API.\n\nAn attacker can arbitrarily reduce the cost of their registration to the minimum payment amount accepted by PayMongo (usually 20 PHP for e-wallets), completely bypassing the actual race entry costs while still receiving a fully paid and confirmed registration.\n\nValidate that `transactionFeeCents` is greater than or equal to 0 before calculating `expectedTotal`. If the client submits a negative transaction fee, reject the request. Additionally, verify in the webhook handler that the amount paid matches the registration's total expected amount, not just relying on the status change.",
                "markdown": "The checkout routes blindly trust the `transactionFee` (or `transactionFeeCents`) value provided by the client when calculating the total order amount. Because the application does not validate that the transaction fee is non-negative, an attacker can submit a negative transaction fee to offset the cost of the event. When choosing an e-wallet like GCash or PayMaya, this manipulated, vastly reduced total amount is passed directly to the PayMongo Payment Intents API.\n\nAn attacker can arbitrarily reduce the cost of their registration to the minimum payment amount accepted by PayMongo (usually 20 PHP for e-wallets), completely bypassing the actual race entry costs while still receiving a fully paid and confirmed registration.\n\nValidate that `transactionFeeCents` is greater than or equal to 0 before calculating `expectedTotal`. If the client submits a negative transaction fee, reject the request. Additionally, verify in the webhook handler that the amount paid matches the registration's total expected amount, not just relying on the status change."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-602",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/602.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "name": "Path Traversal in /api/upload via Unsanitized file.name",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path Traversal in /api/upload via Unsanitized file.name"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `/api/upload` endpoint uses `uploadPublicFile` to store images and templates in Vercel Blob. It passes `file.name` directly from the multipart form data without sanitizing directory traversal sequences (like `../`). An authenticated organizer can place files outside the intended `events/` folder in the public Blob store."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `/api/upload` endpoint uses `uploadPublicFile` to store images and templates in Vercel Blob. It passes `file.name` directly from the multipart form data without sanitizing directory traversal sequences (like `../`). An authenticated organizer can place files outside the intended `events/` folder in the public Blob store.\n\nAn attacker with organizer privileges can write files to arbitrary directories within the public Vercel Blob store. This can pollute other directories (like `avatars/`) and bypass intended directory structuring.\n\nSanitize `file.name` in `uploadPublicFile` by stripping out directory traversal characters (`/` and `\\`), just like it is done in `uploadPrivateProof`. Additionally, override the file extension by relying on the explicitly validated Content-Type rather than trusting the user-provided filename.",
                "markdown": "The `/api/upload` endpoint uses `uploadPublicFile` to store images and templates in Vercel Blob. It passes `file.name` directly from the multipart form data without sanitizing directory traversal sequences (like `../`). An authenticated organizer can place files outside the intended `events/` folder in the public Blob store.\n\nAn attacker with organizer privileges can write files to arbitrary directories within the public Vercel Blob store. This can pollute other directories (like `avatars/`) and bypass intended directory structuring.\n\nSanitize `file.name` in `uploadPublicFile` by stripping out directory traversal characters (`/` and `\\`), just like it is done in `uploadPrivateProof`. Additionally, override the file extension by relying on the explicitly validated Content-Type rather than trusting the user-provided filename."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
