            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-23 01:55:38 UTC",
              "target": "Next.js Middleware (src/proxy.ts)",
              "cwe": "CWE-287",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can bypass the edge middleware authentication layer and reach protected admin routes. While the routes currently have redundant checks (`requireActor`), bypassing middleware exposes the underlying application logic and could lead to unauthorized access if a page misses the manual check.",
              "technical_analysis": "The middleware intended to protect the `/admin/*` routes (currently named `proxy.ts`) uses a vulnerable `startsWith` check to whitelist public paths:\n```typescript\nif (PUBLIC_ADMIN_PATHS.some(path => pathname.startsWith(path))) {\n  return NextResponse.next();\n}\n```\nBecause `request.nextUrl.pathname` retains URL-encoded characters (like `%2f`), an attacker can craft a URI like `/admin/login/..%2f..%2fadmin%2fprotected` which satisfies the `startsWith('/admin/login')` condition. Once passed through the middleware, the Next.js router decodes the URL and normalizes the path traversal, routing the attacker directly to the protected page. This completely defeats the middleware's authentication enforcement. Additionally, the file is named `proxy.ts` instead of `middleware.ts`, causing Next.js to ignore it entirely.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Rename `proxy.ts` to `middleware.ts` so Next.js actually loads the middleware.\n2. Replace `startsWith` with exact path matching, or parse the normalized pathname safely. Next.js provides `request.nextUrl.pathname` which can be strictly checked against the `PUBLIC_ADMIN_PATHS` array.",
              "counterevidence": "The individual pages under `/admin` also check authentication using `requireActor()` or `requireTeamActor()`, so the application has defense-in-depth that mitigates the impact of this middleware bypass. In addition, the file is currently named `proxy.ts`, which Next.js ignores.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If a protected admin page is missing the `requireActor()` check, this bypass would lead to full unauthorized access, increasing the severity to High.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Intercept a request to the application.\n2. Modify the URL to `/admin/login/..%2f..%2fadmin%2fsettings`.\n3. Send the request without any authentication cookies.\n4. The middleware evaluates `pathname.startsWith('/admin/login')` as true and skips checking the `admin_token` cookie, forwarding the request to the Next.js router which resolves it to `/admin/settings`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
